  "tips": {
    "greetings": "💡 \"Olá\" is always safe. \"Bom dia/tarde/noite\" for morning/afternoon/evening. Address strangers politely as \"senhor/senhora\".",
    "food": "💡 Lunch is the main meal — restaurants have excellent \"menu do dia\" (daily set menu) with two courses and drink for ~€12.",
    "directions": "💡 Lisbon's trams are iconic but slow. Use Uber or the Metro for efficiency. Porto is very walkable.",
    "numbers": "💡 Euro (€) is the currency. Tipping ~10% is appreciated but not mandatory.",
    "emergency": "💡 Dial 112 for all emergencies. Portugal is very safe for tourists.",
    "shopping": "Cards are widely accepted. Say olá when you enter a shop.",
    "medical": "Call 112. A pharmacy can help with minor problems.",
    "immigration": "Schengen rules apply. Have the hotel address.",
    "hotel": "Lisbon and Porto are steep. Ask whether you can walk to the room with bags.",
    "transport": "Validate the transit card. Uber and Bolt both work."
  },
  "pronGuide": {
    "title": "European Portuguese Sounds",
    "tips": [
      "Portuguese vowels are often swallowed — unstressed E sounds almost silent (\"de\" = \"d'\", \"que\" = \"k'\")",
      "The LH pair makes a \"ly\" sound and NH makes \"ny\" — like Italian",
      "S between vowels sounds like \"z\"; at end of syllable it becomes \"sh\"",
      "The ã and ão endings are nasal — \"manhã\" (morning) = man-YANG, \"não\" (no) = nowng"
    ]
  },
